{"type":"text","timestamp":1795795009081,"sessionID":"ses_de2479d32ffe8oo7XH9kvyG3sM","part":{"id":"prt_21db93935001L7YCBNM4B9q9G9","sessionID":"ses_de2479d32ffe8oo7XH9kvyG3sM","messageID":"msg_21db92216001WVudp4KHoXLF9J","type":"text","text":"Done. Here is the change I proposed and implemented.\n\n## What changed\n\n**`main.go`** - Added a narrow, single-method interface for integer operations:\n\n```go\ntype IntegerOperation interface {\n\tApply(a, b int) int\n}\n```\n\n- `addOperation` implements it with addition.\n- The existing `add` helper now delegates to `addOperation{}.Apply(a, b)`, so the interface is actually exercised rather than dead code.\n\n**`main_test.go`** - Added a focused test asserting that `addOperation` satisfies `IntegerOperation` and produces the correct result. The original `TestAdd` is unchanged.\n\n## Design note\n\nThe interface is intentionally narrow (one method). It abstracts a binary integer operation without coupling callers to a concrete implementation, so future operations can implement the same interface and be composed or injected. Existing call sites (`add`) are preserved.\n\n## Verification\n\nAll green:\n- `go test ./...` passes\n- `go build ./...` succeeds\n- `go vet ./...` clean\n- `gofmt -l .` reports no unformatted files","time":{"start":1795795007797,"end":1795795009081}}}
